Z||000000||SpeedPhreak||09-16-1999||11:19 PM||||I am buying an 84 tomorrow.  i am probably getting a little stiffed on it but the dealership is financing me & i NEED a car.  i ride a suzuki GSXR600 & i live in colorado, that means snow.  i thought that the other day it was going to snow on me while i was riding.  so i am going to get it.  its $1995 w/a cracked windshield & broken headlight buckets.  <P>what my questions are. as my name implies i am a SPEED PHREAK (not the drug).  i want more power.  i have to have a manual, i am more concerned with how fast i can make it to the top of a pass marked with 35 mph corners than top speed.  so a high reving motor is preferable (kinda like me bike   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">)<P>so turbo quad 4?  6cyl..turbo?  mazda rotary???  <P>ok now it goes fast...time to stop & turn<P>ryan motorsports makes a slalom suspension, any one have or tried one?  2800 bucks on a front suspension is alot especially when it costs more than the car.  i want to use the one with a widend track.  will that work with the stock body work?? i am planning on getting a rebody (aldino or pisa scorpion) next summer.  how about 17 or 18 in wheels with 13" brakes on the stock body.  how much of the suspension/body/engine has to be done together & how much can i do bit by bit?  ideally i would like to wait until i can ride my bike everyday again then do the wheels then the brakes then the suspension then the engine then the body/interior.  & how hard is it to work on these things? only cars ive ever worked on were a 74nova & 74charger.  extent...clutches, oilchanges, & HUGE stereos.  its wierd...i can get a motorcycle in pieces, get is clymer manual (chilton for bikes) put it back together & ride.  cars i cant seem to figure out.  i had to have my dad show me twice how to change a clutch b4 i got it.  any way i appreciate the help.<BR><p>[This message has been edited by SpeedPhreak (edited 09-16-1999).]||||reg||6
Z||000001||ChadMan||09-17-1999||10:28 AM||||The Quad 4 option is intriguing, but they didn't make much torque at all.  Plus the H.O. Quad 4 is the only one worth owning because the others didn't make enough HP to make the swap worth your while.  And the early Quad 4's were notorious for blowing head gaskets, so I don't think pressurizing the intake with a turbo would be a very good idea.<P>If you care more about quick acceleration than top speed, I'd think long and hard about dropping a built-up 2.8 or stock 3.4 into your Fiero and keep the factory 4-speed (if it's the 4.10 "performance" axle, that is).  This engine can easily make 160-180 hp while still being reliable, and it will bolt right up to your existing transaxle and cradle without extensive modifications.<P>I don't know jack about aftermarket Fiero suspensions, other than reading about Ryane Motorsports products in PISA's catalog and thinking "Hmm, those look cool."||||reg||1
Z||000002||batboy||09-17-1999||09:45 PM||||If you do most of your driving in the mountains, you need an engine with lots of torque...forget about the quad 4. The Ryane Motor Sports suspension stuff is mainly for racing, there are much cheaper options for improving handling for street use. For fast mountain driving, I'd look into improving the brakes, that's for sure. A Mazda engine in a Fiero? I don't think so. Way too much money and fabricating. Again, not a lot of torque. You're much better off choosing a GM engine if you're serious about doing an engine swap. Lots of info about swaps in this forum, spend some time looking at old discussions.||||reg||1
